Thanks for checking!

**Conflicts:** yes, both add a helper in the same spot; whichever lands second, I'll rebase it — the resolution is to keep both functions.

**API change:** intentional. I tried exactly that first — keep `io.Writer` and fast-path `*buf.Buffer` via a type assertion, with `buf.StackNew()` as the fallback for other writers. Why I dropped it:
- every one of the 20 callers in the repo already passes a `*buf.Buffer`, so the fallback is dead code here;
- the fallback was slower than `main` for other writers (IPv4 22.4 → 29.4 ns), because of the pool round trip;
- the fast path depends on a runtime type check: any wrapper around `*buf.Buffer` silently falls back to the slow path, while the narrowed signature makes the compiler guarantee it;
- it matches `ReadAddressPort`, which already takes `*buf.Buffer`.

If maintainers prefer to keep `io.Writer` for external users, I can switch back to the fast-path version — it's a small change.

@RPRX, XDrive works as expected with the usual settings. I have a proposal for a small, separate PR for the case where a user explicitly sets the UDP/443 policy and relies on XDrive to fill in `concurrency`.

When XDrive creates the Multiplex block with `concurrency: 128` in [`infra/conf/xray.go`](https://github.com/XTLS/Xray-core/blob/67756b3674d56b72c7a7e675e8dcd3f883e1b2f7/infra/conf/xray.go#L267-L274), copy the original `xudpProxyUDP443` value into it before validation. That preserves `allow` and lets the existing check reject `skip`.

I tried this in a scratch copy of `main`. The diff is **+8/−4 lines** in `infra/conf/xray.go` and **29 lines of tests** in `infra/conf/transport_test.go`. I verified both cases through the CLI.

